
Project 3 — Nashville Housing Data Cleaning (SQL)

Project Objective
The primary goal of this project is to clean a raw dataset using SQL, preparing it for analysis by handling missing values, standardizing formats, removing duplicates, and addressing other data inconsistencies.

Data Source
The data source for this project is a dataset that requires cleaning, likely loaded into a database system (e.g., SQL Server, PostgreSQL, MySQL).

Approach

Converted SaleDate to date only

Repopulated missing PropertyAddress using ParcelId joins across rows

Split Property and Owner addresses into address, city, state using SUBSTRING, CHARINDEX, and PARSENAME

Converted SoldAsVacant bit to Yes/No

Removed duplicates using CTE + ROW_NUMBER

Dropped unused columns for a lean table

Key Results

Address completeness improved without blind deletion

Columns reshaped for cleaner joins and grouping

Duplicates removed, table footprint reduced

Skills Demonstrated
SQL Fundamentals (SELECT, FROM, WHERE, etc.)
Data Cleaning Techniques in SQL
Handling NULL values (IS NULL, IS NOT NULL, COALESCE, etc.)
Data Type Conversion and Casting
String Manipulation Functions (SUBSTRING, PARSENAME, etc.)
Conditional Logic in SQL (CASE statements)
Working with Common Table Expressions (CTEs)
Identifying and Removing Duplicates (ROW_NUMBER() partition by)
Updating and Altering Tables

How to Use the SQL Script
Ensure you have a database system with the dataset loaded.
Execute the queries in the CodeDataCleaning.sql script sequentially in your SQL environment.
Review the results of each query to understand the cleaning steps.
